<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Imperial Clan Court</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1389">1389</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Court of the Imperial Clan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Imperial Clan Court was the institution responsible for all matters concerning the imperial family under the Ming and Qing dynasties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and later the Nguyễn dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Hongwu Emperor</a> established it in 1389, drawing on earlier Tang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> and Yuan offices. Staffed by members of the imperial clan, it came under the Ministry of Rites in the Ming and stood outside the regular bureaucracy in the Qing. Imperial clansmen who committed crimes were not tried in the regular courts. From reports of births, marriages and deaths the Court compiled the imperial genealogy, revised 28 times under the Qing.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The princes, the consorts of imperial princesses and ennobled relatives staffed the <b>Imperial Clan Court</b>, which kept the imperial genealogy.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
